{"name":"Crater","hex":"#75674f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Crater","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A dim spring constellation of the northern sky, said to resemble a cup. It lies between the constellations Virgo and Hydra."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Crater"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crater","blocks":[{"text":"A crater is a landform consisting of a hole or depression on a planetary surface, usually caused either by an object hitting the surface, or by geological activity on the planet. A crater has classically been described as: \"a bowl-shaped pit that is formed by a volcano, an explosion, or a meteorite impact\". On Earth, craters are \"generally the result of volcanic eruptions\", while \"meteorite impact craters are common on the Moon, but are rare on Earth\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Barringer_Crater_aerial_photo_by_USGS.jpg/330px-Barringer_Crater_aerial_photo_by_USGS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crater"},{"title":"Crater (constellation)","blocks":[{"text":"Crater is a small constellation in the southern celestial hemisphere. Its name is the Latinization of the Greek krater, a type of cup used to water down wine. One of the 48 constellations listed by the second-century astronomer Ptolemy, it depicts a cup that has been associated with the god Apollo and is perched on the back of Hydra the water snake."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Crater_IAU.svg/330px-Crater_IAU.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crater_(constellation)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Manicouagan-EO.JPG/500px-Manicouagan-EO.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wik